  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1219 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nDuring the latter half of the work week, the mid-level disturbance\nover NM will gradually lift northeast, with additional embedded \nwaves traversing the southwest flow regime to our west/north. This\nshould result in precip chances spreading eastward compared to \nTues, but will still be highest over the NW FA compared to the \neastern Rolling Plains. For the Wed AM - Sat AM period, NBM \nmembers still depict a meaningful chance (60%) of 2\" of rain in \nthe far SW TX Panhandle (decreasing to 20-30% chance of 2\" along \nI-27). Further east, the rainfall prospects are much less \noptimistic, with just a 50% chance of exceeding 0.25\" of rain \nacross the eastern Rolling Plains (between Wed AM - Sat AM). While\nisolated severe storms can't be ruled out, overall the threat \nappears low owing to weak wind shear and lower cloud bases \nreducing the downdraft strength. Skies remain mostly cloudy \nthrough Friday, with high temps varying from the mid/upper 70s on\nthe Caprock to near 90 in the eastern Rolling Plains.\n\nLate weekend into early next week, guidance favors the upper ridge\naxis shifting back west over the region, with 500 mb heights \naround 588-590 dam. While not as anomalous as some of the other \nrecent upper ridges we've experienced, this will still support a \nwarming trend back to above normal temps with highs in the upper \n80s/low 90s on the Caprock and mid-90s off the Caprock.",
